---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const loading = `let cfg = DotCfg::new("myapp");

// No file and no matching environment values: Ok(None).
let optional: Option<AppConfig> = cfg.load_async().await?;

// Require a source instead of accepting None.
let required: AppConfig = cfg.load_or_error_async().await?;

// Or create and save AppConfig::default() when no source exists.
let ready: AppConfig = cfg.load_or_default_async().await?;`;

const saving = `let config = AppConfig {
    theme: "dark".into(),
};

cfg.save_async(&config).await?;`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Async loading and saving — dotcfg"
  description="Load or save complete configuration values with Tokio-backed file I/O."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">Async I/O</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Async loading & saving
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      In v0.4.0, <code>DotCfg</code> can load and save complete structs with Tokio file I/O. Enable
      the <code>async</code> feature as shown in the{' '}
      <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io">async I/O introduction</a>, then call
      these methods from an async function.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Choose what missing configuration means
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>load_async()</code> returns <code>Ok(None)</code> when neither a file nor matching
      environment values exist. <code>load_or_error_async()</code> returns{' '}
      <code>Error::NotFound</code> instead. <code>load_or_default_async()</code> saves your type's{' '}
      <code>Default</code> value when there is no source. Pick the behavior your application needs.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust, inside an async function" code={loading} />
    <Callout type="info" title="Missing, empty, and malformed remain distinct">
      <p>
        A valid empty file is still an existing source and is deserialized as an empty map. A
        malformed file still returns a parse error. See the{' '}
        <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/loading-saving">loading and saving guide</a>
        for the full behavior.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Save a complete value
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>save_async()</code> serializes the value in the chosen format and writes it to the
      source's config file. The directory is created when needed.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust" code={saving} />
    <p>
      To change one field while preserving the rest of the file, use the methods in{' '}
      <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/key-access">async key access</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      When you have several sources
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>ConfigStack</code> provides <code>load_async()</code>,{' '}
      <code>load_or_error_async()</code>, and <code>save_async()</code> for layered configuration.
      Its save method writes only to the selected source. See{' '}
      <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/config-stack">async ConfigStack</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
